A||||12||8Ball||Still..... Water Pump Woes||||1||
Z||000000||8Ball||09-09-2001||06:06 PM||||Ok as some of you know, I have been trying desperatly to change the water pump on my 86 2m6 for 2 weeks now. I have been stuck on 1 nut at the top one small bolt behind the water pump pulley and 1 small bolt behind the crank pulley. Well I tried again to get the nut off and well it came off as the bolt broke  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I still cannot get the other 2 but if I ever do can it work without that one nut or do I need to stop now and leave my car sitting til I can afford to have someone fix the water pump and drill out the broken bolt ect ect ect. And if so About how much am I looking to spend?<P>Thanks,<BR>Randy||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||09-09-2001||06:15 PM||||Once you get the pump off, pliers should get the bolt out without much problem.<P>Did you take the pulley off before starting this task? I always remove the pulley first, makes for alot more room to work.<P>remove battery<BR>remove upper bolts<BR>Remove driver rear wheel<BR>remove wheel-well shield<BR>remove lower bolts<P>installation is reverse.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||8Ball||09-09-2001||06:19 PM||||I did not take off the pulley as I cannot get it off. And on my car the water pump is on the passenger side   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>Oh yea the bolt seems to have broken off deep inside the block as I got about a 2.5 inch long piece out<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 8Ball (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||09-09-2001||06:44 PM||||None of the bosses on the waterpump are threaded. Just reef the rest of the bolts out. The block's cas iron, and pretty hard, so it shouldn't be too much of a hassle to get some EZouts in when the pump's off. I would recommend a sound drenching wit ED-40, and hit the head of the bolt with a hammer, and rock it back and forth on a large lever, until ot works loose. USE LOTS OF WD-40. It'll come out eventually. Good luck!||||reg||4
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||09-09-2001||06:59 PM||||Why is you cant take the pulley off? are those bolts rusted also?And the water pump is passenger side on everybodys side here. And mach's idea seems like the best plan.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||09-09-2001||07:26 PM||||One more thing. If you can find one, get a short 6" angle bar(looks like ratchet but without ratcheting head) and put on an impac socket, and start hitting it with a hammer. Don't hit too hard, but don't feather tap either. I've had good luck getting frozen bolts/nuts off with this method. Doesn't put much torque on the bolt to break it, but it'll loosen things up. bear in mind it might take a while. And impac gun with long series of wobble extensions would be the best bet, but do make do with what you have.<BR>You CAN use a regular ratchet, but this method tends to destroy the ratcheting mechanism...||||reg||7
Z||000006||8Ball||09-09-2001||07:34 PM||||As far as the pulley wheel I am haveing a hard time holding it still. I have tried putting a wrench on one bolt and a rachet on another and turning against the 2 to try to hold it but thus far I can't break the bolts free.<P>Looks Like I need a mechanic who will work for beer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||09-09-2001||07:41 PM||||Get me a ticket to TN, and a MO for some REAL beer (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) and I'll have it apart in no time. That is, if you got lots of bandaids, maybe a couple of knuckle-skin grafts, and a tolerance for some blistering cuss words...<P><BR>Relax, have a beer, don't even LOOK at the car for an hour. Then take a big hammer, and follow my previous directions and tap them out. Technically, with only one bolt broken, you wouldn't even need to have it drilled out. the silicon should seal it fine with all the rest of the fasteners in place... But do that at your own risk  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>As for steadying the pully, I've always just loosened the bolts before taking the belt off. One solid whack with a hammer on a prybar usually does it for each one.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000008||8Ball||09-09-2001||07:52 PM||||Ok guys, I am going to try to salvage the last bit of daylight and try to get those last 2 bolts off<P>Thanks everyone<BR>Randy||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||09-09-2001||09:15 PM||||hand held impact wrench the kind you hit with a hammer is a good tool if you have room to use it cheap too all most never breaks bolts.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||09-09-2001||09:20 PM||||I meant passenger side, not driver side like it came out.<P>I find a hammer and wrench always get the pulley bolts off without much problem.||||reg||1
Z||000011||b lo 0||09-09-2001||11:41 PM||||That's funny, I got mine out with a peice of gum and a Twinkie.  Not really.  Sure, it's a PITA to get out, but I had nowhere near the problems you're having.  Penetrating lube and a good breaker bar are necessities in cast-iron blocks.  Was your battery leaking at some point?  The corrosion from battery acid could have caused the bolts to freeze.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">       <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com.com" TARGET=_blank>Audio Imagery</A>, budget-priced car audio supplies from people like Monster Cable, Sony & more!||||reg||1
Z||000012||JayUSA77||09-10-2001||01:05 AM||||use that liquid wrench stuff, its better than WD-40 that might help.<BR>J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
